address = {New York, NY, USA},
abstract = {Welcome to the Thirteenth (13th) ACM International Conference on Systems for Energy Efficient Buildings, Cities, and Transportation (BuildSys'26), in Banff, Canada! Over the past years, BuildSys has facilitated important discussions and developments in the convergence of computer science and disciplines related to the built environment, including engineering, architecture, urban planning, and social science. This year's conference marks the first time we have joined forces with ACM e-Energy to organize two co-located conferences as part of the inaugural ACM Sustainability Week 2026. BuildSys and e-Energy represent separate but complementary research communities that can mutually benefit from closer interaction. ACM BuildSys generally emphasizes optimizations at the edge of the energy system—the buildings, cities, and transportation systems that consume energy—and thus has strong inter-sections with both civil and mechanical engineering. ACM e-Energy generally emphasizes optimizing the energy network, including power generation, transmission, and distribution systems, and thus has historically had strong intersections with the power systems community and electrical engineering. In essence, BuildSys and e-Energy both apply computational techniques to optimize energy use and reduce carbon emissions, but often from different perspectives and using different approaches.},
